=U(O)\nGraph G: S → A, S → Y, maybe S → X if predictor decides what observation you receive; Θ → X, Θ → O; Y → X, Y → O, etc. Intervention do(S=π) sets Π=π, propagates through Y, X, A, O. Value V(π)=E[V | do(S=π)].\nCould write expected utility equation:\nP-FDT(P,G) ∈ argmax_{π∈Π} ∑_o P(o | do(S=π); G) U(o).\nThen actual action: a* = π*(x). If sequential histories h_t, π: H→A; choose π*; at history h, do π*(h).\nMaybe add if conditioning on exogenous evidence e:\nP-FDT_e(P,G) ∈ argmax_π E[V | do(S=π), E=e] where E variables are certified exogenous with respect to S. But caution.\n\nNeed examples with equations. Let's prepare.\n\nExample 1: Newcomb with no observation. Policies: π_1 (one-box), π_2 (two-box). Variable Fill depends on predictor's prediction of π; P(Full | do(S=π_1))=.99, P(Full | do(S=π_2))=.01. Utility: U(one,full)=1,000,000; U(two,full)=1,001,000; U(one,empty)=0; U(two,empty)=1,000. Compute:\nV(π_1)=.99*1,000,000 + .01*0 = 990,000.\nV(π_2)=.01*1,001,000 + .99*1,000 = 11,000.\nChoose π_1.\nIf actual no obs, one-box.\n\nExample 2: Smoking lesion. Policies smoke/refrain. Lesion L has prior p, affects utility? In source: lesion causes love smoking and cancer. There are two utility functions; but simple: smoking gives +1000, cancer -1,000,000? Source values: smoking utility $1000, no cancer $1,000,000. Lesion causes cancer 99%; no lesion 1%. It also causes preference for smoking. P-FDT intervention on policy does not change L. So expected difference smoking vs refrain is +1000 times probability policy relevant? Source more nuanced: if lesion determines whether policy controlling action is P_S or P_R. But for simple operational, if policy can control action regardless lesion? If lesion causes love smoking but agent still decides? Need maybe simpler: P-FDT sees no subjunctive dependency from policy to cancer; smoking adds $1000 no effect on cancer probability; choose smoke. To align source, can say if lesion determines which utility function, P-FDT evaluate